WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.400
Let’s look at the spine, and the SI joints.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.620
Axial disease means PsA affects the spine.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:15.690
It may also affect the sacroiliac, or SI, joints, which link the lower spine to the pelvis.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:22.450
Inflammation here can cause stiffness in the neck, the lower back, and the hips.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.460
Stiffness is often worse in the morning, or after resting.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.640
And it may get better with exercise.

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:36.010
Tell your care team where your back feels sore or stiff.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.180
Note when it happens, and how it affects sleep or movement.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:46.700
Back pain can have many causes, and a map cannot show which one you have.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:53.840
A clinician uses your story, an exam, and sometimes tests, to understand the pattern.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.830
Imaging can show changes in the spine.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:03.800
Low-impact exercise, like walking, swimming, yoga, or tai chi, may help.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.380
Talk with your doctor before you start.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.450
Great job! Let’s review.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.220
Back pain can have many causes.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.740
These joints link the lower spine to the pelvis.
